# {{project.name}}

A kernel starting point for QEMU's RISC-V `virt` machine, with no C library.

```sh
mcpp run
```

## What is beneath this project

Nothing but the compiler, the instruction set, and the board package. The
manifest declines the target's C library with `sysroot = ""`, so `<stdio.h>`
does not resolve and no startup object runs before `_start`.

The board package is depended upon with `features = ["nolibc"]`, which selects
the half of it that needs no C library: the memory map, a console that writes
the UART directly, and the emulator to run under. The project therefore contains
two files — this one and an entry point.

## The other starting point

`mcpp new <name> --template riscv-virt-rt:nolibc` targets the same machine and
depends on nothing at all: it carries its own linker script, writes the UART
address into its source, and names the emulator in its manifest. That is the
accurate picture of what a kernel requires, and the better one to read first.

The difference between the two is not what the program does. It is how much of
this board's datasheet is copied into the project.

## Adding to it

| you want | add |
| --- | --- |
| `memcpy`, `memset` and the other functions the compiler may emit | `std-freestanding-nolibc` |
| the freestanding subset of the C++ standard library | `std-freestanding` with `features = ["nolibc"]` |
| `std::vector`, `std::string` and the rest of the allocating half | the above, plus `features = ["alloc-kal"]` and an openkal implementation |
